import DocPage from "../layouts/DocPage.astro";
import { companyFacts } from "../lib/companyProfile";
---

<DocPage
    pageTitle="Manufacturing"
    description="How MEIYU forms, perforates, coats and packs aluminium ceiling and facade panels in Guangdong — alloys, processes and tolerances."
    titleWhite="Manufacturing"
    titleGold="Capability"
    eyebrow="What happens in the plant"
    subtitle="The processes between your drawings and a packed container."
>
    <ul class="doc-facts">
        <li><b>{companyFacts.established}</b><span>Established</span></li>
        <li><b>{companyFacts.plantArea}</b><span>Plant area</span></li>
        <li>
            <b>{companyFacts.productionLines}</b><span>Production lines</span>
        </li>
        <li><b>{companyFacts.staff}</b><span>People</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it happens</h2>
    <p>
        All forming, perforation, coating and packing take place in our own
        plant in
        {companyFacts.location}. We buy coil and sheet from certified mills and
        process it in-house rather than outsourcing the stages that decide
        appearance and dimensional accuracy.
    </p>

    <h2>Materials we work with</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Alloy and temper</strong> — the common ceiling grades (such as
            AA1100 and AA3003) and the structural grades used for facade panels and
            tubes, chosen per product family. The grade we quote is stated on the
            quotation; we do not substitute within a grade family.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Formats</strong> — coil and sheet for panels and strips, extruded
            profile for baffles, tubes and carriers.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Everything is aluminium.</strong> Subframes and carriers supplied
            with a system may be galvanised steel; fasteners we recommend are stainless
            to avoid galvanic corrosion. Where aluminium must be isolated from carbon
            steel, we say so on the detail.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Processes</h2>
    <h3>Forming</h3>
    <p>
        Roll forming for strip and linear ceiling profiles, press braking and
        folding for panel edges and cassettes, and punching or roll perforation
        for acoustic patterns. Curved and tapered work is formed to your
        setting-out drawing.
    </p>

    <h3>Perforation</h3>
    <p>
        Round, square and slotted patterns are produced to a specified pitch and
        open area. Perforation is what makes an acoustic ceiling behave
        acoustically, so the pattern, aperture and open-area figure are
        confirmed before production — our <a
            href="/tools/perforation-open-area/">open area calculator</a
        > explains how those numbers relate.
    </p>

    <h3>Coating</h3>
    <p>
        Powder coating and PVDF (fluorocarbon) liquid paint, plus anodised and
        wood grain finishes where specified. Exterior panels intended for UV,
        coastal or polluted exposure are quoted on the coating system
        appropriate to that environment. The
        <a href="/surface-treatment-comparison/">surface treatment comparison</a
        >
        sets out how the options differ in practice.
    </p>

    <h3>Assembly and packing</h3>
    <p>
        Cut-to-length members, carriers and suspension hardware are kitted
        against the setting-out drawing so a site crew receives what a given
        zone needs, rather than one bulk bundle. Perimeter trim is quoted as a
        linear metre run, not a piece count.
    </p>

    <h2>Quality control</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Incoming coil and sheet checked against the mill certificate.</li>
        <li>
            First-article dimensional and appearance sign-off before a batch
            runs.
        </li>
        <li>Coating thickness and adhesion checks during the coating stage.</li>
        <li>
            Colour and finish approved against a physical sample panel before
            volume production.
        </li>
        <li>Final visual and dimensional check before packing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we need from you to start</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>System type and product family (or a photo / reference detail).</li>
        <li>Panel dimensions, gauge and alloy preference if you have one.</li>
        <li>
            Finish system and colour reference (RAL, Pantone or a physical
            sample).
        </li>
        <li>
            Perforation pattern and open area, if acoustic performance is
            required.
        </li>
        <li>Quantities by zone, and the destination port or site.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        If you have drawings, send them. If you only have a concept, send that
        too — a sketch and a target budget are usually enough for us to come
        back with options.
    </p>

    <p class="doc-updated">
        Capability summary · exact grades, tolerances and coating systems are
        confirmed with your quotation ·
        <a href="/contact/">Talk to us about a project</a>
    </p>
</DocPage>
